Isomorphic groups are "the same group with renamed elements", so any property defined only from the group operation is shared. To prove two groups are not isomorphic, find a property one has and the other lacks:
the order ∣G∣;
being abelian or cyclic;
the number of elements of each order;
the size of the center, the number of subgroups of a given order, and so on.
Z/4Z has an element of order 4; in Z/2Z×Z/2Z every non-identity element has order 2. So they are not isomorphic, although both have order 4 and are abelian. On the other hand Z/6Z≅Z/2Z×Z/3Z via k↦(k mod 2,k mod 3); this is the Chinese Remainder Theorem from post 00. In general Z/mZ×Z/nZ is cyclic if and only if gcd⁡(m,n)=1.
Watch out: to prove two groups are isomorphic you must produce a map and check it. Matching every invariant you can think of is evidence, not proof.
